Addendum: reusing files from this repository
---------------------------------------------

This addendum states, in plain terms, what you must do to stay compliant
when you copy, port, or redistribute file/s from buzzs-rust-php-project
(https://github.com/davidbuzz/buzzs-rust-php-project). It restates the
BSD-3-Clause conditions above; where it is simpler than the license text,
the license text governs.

1. Keep the notices. Every source file in this repository carries a
   two-line copyright header:

       // buzzs-rust-php-project - Copyright (c) 2025-2026 David Buzz <davidbuzz@gmail.com>

   If you copy a file (whole, modified, or ported to another language),
   keep that header in the copy, and ship the full BSD-3-Clause text
   above (the LICENSE file) with your distribution. For source
   redistributions the header and license text must be in the files and
   the distribution; for binary-only redistributions, reproduce the
   copyright notice and this list of conditions in your documentation
   or equivalent (an About/Licenses page, for example).

2. Say what is yours. You do not need to mark unchanged copies, but you
   may not present copied or derived files as your original work. If you
   modify a file, you may add your own copyright notice on top of the
   existing one — the existing notice stays.

3. Don't use the name. Neither the name of the project
   (buzzs-rust-php-project), nor "David Buzz", may be used to endorse or
   promote products derived from this software without prior written
   permission. Saying you built something that uses or was derived from
   this project, factually, is fine.

4. Third-party files travel under their own licenses. Some files in this
   repository are ports or copies of PHP/Zend sources and carry PHP-3.01
   or Zend copyright notices instead of (or alongside) the BSD header.
   The BSD grant above does not cover them; if you reuse those files,
   you must comply with the PHP License 3.01 (or the notice in the file)
   yourself. The same applies to the test tree under modules/php-src
   (PHP-3.01, © The PHP Group and Contributors), tools/lcov
   (GPL-2.0-or-later), and linked system libraries. When in doubt, the
   header at the top of each file states which license covers it.

5. No warranty, no liability. The software comes as-is, exactly as the
   disclaimer above says; you are responsible for what you do with it.

There is no requirement to contribute changes back, no copyleft, and no
fee. Attribution of the notices above is the whole of the compliance
burden.

---

Third-party components retain their own licenses and are not covered by the
grant above. Notably:

- modules/php-src (the upstream PHP source tree, used as the compatibility
  oracle and test suite): PHP-3.01, Copyright © 1999–2026 The PHP Group and
  Contributors.
- tools/lcov: GPL-2.0-or-later.
- Linked system libraries (PCRE2, libxml2, libzip, OpenSSL, ICU, libmagic,
  libsodium, SQLite, zlib, bzip2): their respective licenses.
